\begin{theorem}[Symmetry-breaking cost table]
Let $p\ne q$ be distinct odd primes, $N=pq$, and $S$ a finite set of distinct odd primes. Then simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\min\IC(S)=\lceil\log_2|S|\rceil$;
\item for every $r\in S$ there is $M$ with $r\mid M$, $\Ker(M)=\Ker(N)$, and $J(a\mid M)=J(a\mid N)$ for all $a$ coprime to $MN$;
\item there exists $x$ with $\gcd(x-1,N)=p$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) is Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}; (2) is Theorem~\ref{thm:zero} applied to each $r$; (3) is Theorems~\ref{thm:exists}--\ref{thm:gcd}.
\end{proof}

The gap between (1) and (2) is the symmetry-breaking cost; (3) is what the quantum channel pays for.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Information-sufficient, computation-sealed}

Quadratic residue data about $N$ is \emph{information-sufficient but computation-sealed}. Sufficient, because $\lceil\log_2\pi(\sqrt N)\rceil\approx\tfrac12\log_2 N$ well-chosen bits determine the factor uniquely and a single gcd witness determines it outright. Sealed, because the publicly computable version of the same symbols has exactly zero pruning power, with an explicit conspiring modulus for each candidate. The seal is a structural identity, not a statistical estimate.

\subsection{What is refuted, and what is unified}

The tempting attack --- compute many symbols $\jac{a_i}{N}$, eliminate candidates, iterate --- is refuted definitively: no candidate is ever eliminated, because each has a modulus with an identical battery. The classical uniform, hint-free residue surface is exhausted.

Two frontiers become one. Classical factoring is expensive because each symmetric relation has zero individual pruning power, so only a factor-base-sized aggregate breaks the tie. The quantum channel buys not information --- the information is already present --- but \emph{pointing}: an asymmetric readout that resolves composite structure instead of projecting it to a single symbol. Both are payments for the same asymmetry, in different currencies; the value of a symmetry-breaking resource is exactly the aggregation it lets one skip.

\subsection{Modelling caveats}

The exact $\log_2$ statement depends on admissibility: without it the alphabet is ternary and pigeonhole weakens to $\log_3|S|$, but $J(a\mid r)=0$ means $r\mid a$, a disclosure rather than an ordinary answer. Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} genuinely needs $M,N$ odd: at $p=2$ there is no quadratic nonresidue and a mod-$8$ argument would be required. No result is vacuous: the isolating battery is exhibited, the compensating modulus is $Nr^2$, and the square-root witness is $x=4,13,10,6,43,12$ for $(p,q)=(3,5),(3,7),(3,11),(5,7),(7,11),(11,13)$, with $\gcd(x-1,pq)=3,3,3,5,7,11$ respectively.

\subsection{Algorithmic content}

\emph{Battery construction.} Given odd primes $S$: fix an injective code $C:S\to\{0,1\}^k$ with $k=\lceil\log_2|S|\rceil$; for each coordinate $i$ and each $p$, take $b_{p,i}=1$ if $C(p)_i=1$ and otherwise a quadratic nonresidue mod $p$ (expected two trials); CRT-combine into $a_i$. Cost $O(k|S|)$ modular operations plus $k$ reconstructions.

\emph{Kernel-blindness certificate.} Given $N$ and a candidate $r$, output $M=Nr^2$: a directly checkable certificate that $r$ cannot be eliminated.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Cost $\lceil\log_m|S|\rceil$ for every multiplicative-character battery]
Replace the quadratic symbol by the $m$-th power residue symbol for arbitrary $m\ge2$, or by any family of Dirichlet characters of bounded conductor. The least battery size isolating a candidate set $S$ of primes $\equiv 1 \bmod m$ is exactly $\lceil\log_m|S|\rceil$ --- the alphabet size, not the arithmetic, sets the price --- and the symmetric battery again determines only the ``kernel'' of $N$ in the character group. Both halves of Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} are alphabet counting plus Chinese remainder surjectivity, and neither uses quadraticity; only local surjectivity of the $m$-th power symbol is needed.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Randomization does not help]
Model a randomized strategy as a distribution over decision trees identifying each candidate with probability $\ge 2/3$. The expected depth is still $\log_2|S|-O(1)$, with constant loss at most $2$. Yao's principle converts the randomized bound into a distributional deterministic bound, and the uniform distribution on $S$ is already hard because Theorem~\ref{thm:indep} makes every answer pattern equally realizable.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Kernel faithfulness at the prime $2$]
With the Kronecker symbol in place of the Jacobi symbol, Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} extends to all nonzero $M,N$ provided kernels are compared in $\mathbb{Q}^\times/(\mathbb{Q}^\times)^2$: the batteries agree on all coprime numerators iff $M$ and $N$ differ by a square, with the classes of $-1$ and $2$ handled by a mod-$8$ analysis.
\end{conjecture}

Further: quantify the quantum side in the same units, asking whether an order-finding oracle realizes exactly the symmetry-breaking bit that the product map destroys; extend the kernel theorem to higher-degree residue symbols and to number fields, where the kernel becomes a class in a ray class group modulo $m$-th powers; and study partial symmetry breaking, using batteries evaluated at $N$ and at auxiliary moduli $N+c$.

\section{Conclusion}

Three exact statements, one picture. The asymmetric residue oracle isolates a hidden prime factor at cost exactly $\lceil\log_2|S|\rceil$, matching the information floor adaptively or not, because the Chinese Remainder Theorem makes local quadratic behaviour completely free. The symmetric public battery of $N$ is a faithful invariant of the squarefree kernel and blind to everything else, so it prunes exactly zero candidates: each candidate $r$ hides inside $Nr^2$. And a witness that factors $N$ with a single gcd always exists, arises from the same freedom, and admits no decoys. The distance between the first and the second is the symmetry-breaking cost; classical algorithms pay it by aggregation and quantum order finding by asymmetric readout, and naming the price lets one compare them on a single scale.

